The African Union’s Fear Is Not Somaliland — It Is the Mirror

The African Union (AU) is reportedly unsettled by Israel’s recognition of Somaliland. But the anxiety unfolding in Addis Ababa has less to do with Somaliland itself and far more to do with unresolved fractures within AU member states. When leaders bristle at recognition, what they are often protecting is not continental unity, but domestic fragility.

At the heart of the AU’s discomfort lies a familiar fear: precedent. If Somaliland is recognized, what happens to secessionist movements elsewhere? Names quickly surface — Ambazonia in Cameroon, Biafra in Nigeria — as if Somaliland were a contagion rather than a case. Yet this framing obscures a fundamental truth: secessionist movements do not emerge because others are recognized; they emerge because states fail their citizens.

Somaliland did not arise from chaos or armed rebellion. It reclaimed the sovereignty it briefly held in 1960, built functioning institutions, held competitive elections, maintained internal peace, and governed without international aid dependency. For more than three decades, it has demonstrated what the AU often claims to champion: African solutions rooted in stability, consent, and self-governance.

By contrast, movements like Ambazonia and Biafra are symptoms of unresolved national questions — marginalization, political exclusion, broken federal promises, and the absence of justice. Blaming Somaliland for their existence is like blaming a mirror for the cracks it reflects.

Israel’s recognition of Somaliland may be geopolitically significant, but it does not invent international law. Self-determination is enshrined in the UN Charter and African legal instruments alike. What unsettles some African governments is not legality, but accountability. Recognition forces a question many prefer to avoid: why do some African polities hold together only by force?

The AU was born to overcome the injustices of arbitrary borders imposed in Berlin in 1885. Yet today, it often defends those borders more fiercely than the people trapped within them. Territorial integrity has become a shield behind which repression hides comfortably.

If African states treated minorities justly, governed inclusively, and honored constitutional bargains, secessionist movements would indeed wither — not because they are banned, but because they are unnecessary. Unity built on coercion is not unity; it is deferred crisis.

Somaliland is not Africa’s problem. It is Africa’s test. And the real question is not why some seek independence, but why so many feel they have no other choice.
